A dereference of an address is an lvalue -- and this is what tcc was stopping on.

MICRO-C.md names `++*(p)` in tcc's TOK_GET as the remaining fault and attributes
it to pointer arithmetic that does not scale. The shape is right, the cause is
not: scaling was fixed in the previous patch and the marker trail did not move
by one statement. Three separate defects sit in that one macro.

    #define TOK_GET(t, p, c) do { \
        int _t = **(p); \
        if (TOK_HAS_VALUE(_t)) tok_get(t, p, c); \
        else *(t) = _t, ++*(p); \
        } while (0)                                     tccpp.c:1245

It is reached as TOK_GET(&t, &macro_str, &cval), so after substitution the
expressions are literally `**(&macro_str)`, `*(&t) = _t` and `++*(&macro_str)`.
Every one of them is a dereference of a PARENTHESISED expression, because that
is how you write a macro parameter safely, and every one of them was wrong.

(1) THE PREFIX ++ TEST COULD NOT SEE ITSELF. is_prefix_operator looked two
    tokens back from the identifier. That is the `++` for `++p` and the `*` for
    `++*p`, so the whole through-a-dereference class took the ordinary rvalue
    path, loaded one level too far, and incremented the pointed-AT object
    instead of the pointer. Reading it from the token in front of the STARS --
    remembered before the loop eats them -- covers both. `(*p)++` was never
    affected: the operator sits after the expression, where nothing has been
    consumed yet.

(2) AN ASSIGNMENT THROUGH PARENTHESES STORED THROUGH THE LOADED VALUE.

        *(t) = 7;      SIGSEGV
        *t  = 7;       correct, and four characters shorter

    This is the eighth copy of the `assigning` rule in cc_core.c and the third
    one found missing. It is the defect that was actually killing tcc.

(3) `*(&x)` IS `x`, AND THE TYPE HAS TO SAY SO. `&x` reports x's OWN type, one
    level short of what it is -- there is no `T*` handed back. So the
    dereference stepped the type down from a level that was never stepped up
    and landed one below where it started, and `++*(&macro_str)` added ONE
    instead of the element size. The address was right the whole time.

    Note which application cancels the `&`: the FIRST, not the last. Keying it
    off the loop's final iteration is right only when there is exactly one
    star, so `++*(&x)` came out correct in isolation while tcc -- which writes
    `**(p)` in the same macro -- did not move at all. That cost a round.

FOUND IN A MINUTE, NOT A ROUND, and the method is the point. `difftest.sh`
reduced TOK_GET to fifteen lines of self-contained C, and bisecting it locally
separated `*(t) = 7` from `*t = 7` in about a second. Reading emitted assembly
would have found the extra load; it would not have told me which of the three
constructs in the macro carried it.

MEASURED, both architectures, patched tables:

    amd64     pass 45  fail 0    cases 21 and 48 both closed
    aarch64   pass 44  fail 1    16-switch-wide is PRE-EXISTING (see zz7)
    regression.sh                0 regressions

AND THE FRONTIER MOVED, which is the first movement past the fault MICRO-C.md
documents:

    before   ... P97 P100 P115 P116 P119 [SIGSEGV]
    after    ... P97 P100 P115 P116 P119 P100 P65 [SIGSEGV]

P100 is `TOK_GET(&t, &macro_str, &cval)`. It now completes a SECOND time --
macro_subst's loop body iterates -- and control returns into macro_subst_tok,
where P65 (`ret = macro_subst(tok_str, nested_list, jstr)`, tccpp.c:3396)
completes before the fault. 21 markers to 23.

The new fault is AFTER macro_subst returns, around tok_str_free_str. It has not
been diagnosed. Instrument macro_subst_tok's tail next.

